/* Sober palette for the jaull TUI.

   Everything below is built from the tokens declared first. A colour literal
   anywhere else in this file is a bug: it means a concept escaped the palette.

   The house style is an instrument, not a dashboard. Rules and whitespace do
   the separating; a border is reserved for what is genuinely modal or
   genuinely selected. Density comes from alignment, not from removing air.

   Border vocabulary
   -----------------
   Nothing is enclosed in a rounded rectangle any more. An edge means one of
   four things, and its weight says which:

        tinted row     selected — the row you act on
     ▌  outer left     a callout beside the content: a warning, a command
     ─  solid bottom   a field you can type in, or a zone rule
     ━  heavy bottom   that field has focus
     ▊…▎ panel         a surface genuinely on top of the page: modals, the
                       open dropdown. The solid top bar reads as a window.

   These are block-element glyphs. They render on every terminal this app
   targets, and each distinction is carried by shape as well as colour, so a
   monochrome terminal loses nothing.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   Tokens.

   Text hierarchy — three levels, used consistently:
     $ink      titles, model names, answers, response bodies
     $ink-2    subtitles, metadata lines, footer
     $ink-3    timings, hints, pending steps, CLI labels

   Colour carries one meaning each. Four is the budget; a fifth state must
   reuse one of these rather than introduce a new hue.

   Names avoid Textual's reserved theme variables. `$text` and `$panel` in
   particular are built-ins that expand to a colour *plus a percentage*, and
   shadowing them breaks Textual's own stylesheet (`hatch: right $panel`),
   which is why the tokens here are `$ink` and `$chrome`.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* Original vivid terminal colours; surfaces stay dark beneath the data. */
$bg:         #070a10;
$surface:    #0d131c;
$surface-2:  #0b111a;
$surface-3:  #16202e;
$chrome:     #0a1018;
$line:       #1b2735;
$line-2:     #274156;
$well:       #04070c;

$ink:        #d8e6f0;
$ink-2:      #8fa8bd;
$ink-3:      #8399ab;

/* One vivid accent and three semantic states. */
$accent:     #00e5ff;
$accent-lit: #7df9ff;
$accent-deep: #0091a7;
$ok:         #00ff9c;
$warn:       #ffc400;
$warn-lit:   #ffe066;
$bad:        #ff2e63;

/* Cyan needs dark text for readable primary actions. */
$on-accent:  #04121a;

/* Tinted grounds for badges and warning panels. Dark enough that the
   foreground token keeps its contrast on top. */
$ok-tint:    #002419;
$warn-tint:  #241c00;
$bad-tint:   #2a0713;
$warn-well:  #14100a;

/* The logo burns the accent; its shimmer fades toward the deep end. */
$logo:       #00e5ff;
$logo-dim:   #0091a7;

Screen {
    background: $bg;
    color: $ink;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   Layout baseline.

   Textual's Vertical, Horizontal and ListView all default to `height: 1fr`.
   Document containers inside scroll regions should follow their content,
   otherwise a single block expands
   to the whole viewport and pushes everything after it below the fold. Size
   them to their content instead; the surrounding VerticalScroll is what
   flexes. Selection workspaces explicitly override this for their panes.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
VerticalScroll > Vertical,
VerticalScroll > Horizontal,
Vertical > Vertical,
Vertical > Horizontal,
ListView {
    height: auto;
}

Header {
    background: $chrome;
    color: $ink;
    text-style: bold;
    height: 1;
    border-bottom: solid $line;
    display: none;
}

/* The default header icon is U+2B58, which most terminal fonts lack and which
   renders as a replacement box. Nothing here depends on it. */
HeaderIcon {
    display: none;
}

Footer {
    background: $chrome;
    color: $ink-2;
}

Footer > .footer--key {
    color: $accent;
    text-style: bold;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   Text hierarchy.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.text-secondary { color: $ink-2; }

/* Secondary information, not decoration: readable, but clearly below the
   primary level. */
.text-muted {
    color: $ink-3;
    padding: 0 0 1 0;
}

/* Same colour, no trailing row: muted text inside a tight block rather than
   closing one. */
.text-muted-tight {
    color: $ink-3;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   Structure. A section is a title and whitespace — the default grouping, and
   the one to reach for before any bordered surface.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.section {
    height: auto;
    margin: 0 0 1 0;
}

.section-title {
    color: $ink;
    text-style: bold;
    padding: 0 0 1 0;
}

/* An eyebrow above a block: short, accented, uppercase at the call site. */
.eyebrow {
    color: $accent;
    text-style: bold;
}

/* One hairline, for separating zones (header, composer). */
.rule {
    border-bottom: solid $line;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   Metrics.

   A measured value earns a real two-column row: label left, value right, so a
   column of numbers lines up instead of relying on ljust inside one string.
   Both halves stay separate Static widgets.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.metric {
    height: auto;
    layout: horizontal;
}

/* `1fr` alone is what broke the old alternatives row: beside fixed-width
   siblings it collapsed to one column and wrapped a repo id one letter per
   line. A floor plus nowrap makes a narrow terminal truncate instead. */
.metric-label {
    color: $ink-2;
    width: 1fr;
    min-width: 12;
    text-wrap: nowrap;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
}

.metric-value {
    color: $ink;
    width: auto;
    max-width: 70%;
    text-align: right;
    text-wrap: nowrap;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
}

/* A value with no evidence behind it reads quieter than a measured one. */
.metric-value.-estimated {
    color: $ink-3;
}

.metric-value.-measured {
    color: $ink;
    text-style: bold;
}

/* Bars never replace the number; they sit beside it in the same row. */
.metric-bar {
    color: $accent;
    width: auto;
    padding: 0 1 0 0;
}

/* A sub-heading inside a metric block: "Prompt processing", "Generation".
   Textual has no sibling combinators, so the rows under it carry `-indented`
   rather than being selected relative to the heading. */
.metric-group {
    color: $ink-2;
    text-style: bold;
}

/* Space between groups, not above the first one — the section title already
   provides that gap. */
.metric-group.-spaced {
    padding: 1 0 0 0;
}

.metric.-indented {
    padding: 0 0 0 2;
}

/* A caveat on how to read the numbers above — present, not shouting. The
   amber warning panel would outweigh the measurements it qualifies. */
.comparison-note {
    color: $warn;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   Evidence vocabulary.

   Four states, ordered by how much they are worth trusting. Each carries a
   glyph at the call site as well, so the distinction survives a monochrome
   terminal and colour-blind readers.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.status-estimated   { color: $ink-3; }
.status-ready       { color: $ink-2; }
.status-validated   { color: $ok; }
.status-benchmarked { color: $ok; text-style: bold; }

.status-ok    { color: $ok;   text-style: bold; }
.status-warn  { color: $warn; text-style: bold; }
.status-fail  { color: $bad;  text-style: bold; }

/* The progress line every long-running screen owns. It was applied on four
   screens and defined nowhere, so it inherited whatever the id rule said. */
.status-line {
    color: $ink-2;
    height: auto;
    margin: 0 0 1 0;
}

.warning-line {
    color: $warn;
    padding: 0 0 0 1;
}

.reason-line {
    color: $ok;
    padding: 0 0 0 1;
}

.fit-good    { color: $ok; }
.fit-warn    { color: $warn; }
.fit-bad     { color: $bad; }
.fit-unknown { color: $ink-3; }

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   Selection.

   A tinted row and bold text keep selection clear without shifting content.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.selectable {
    height: auto;
    padding: 0 1;
}

.selectable:hover {
    background: $surface-2;
}

.selectable.-selected {
    background: $surface-3;
    text-style: bold;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   Chips: compact, inline filter controls. A chip is a Button, so it keeps
   keyboard focus for free; the active one is filled rather than outlined.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.chip {
    height: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    padding: 0 2;
    margin: 0 1 0 0;
    background: transparent;
    color: $ink-3;
    border: none;
}

.chip:hover {
    color: $ink-2;
}

.chip:focus {
    color: $ink;
    text-style: underline;
}

.chip.-active {
    background: $accent;
    color: $on-accent;
    text-style: bold;
}

.chip.-active:hover,
.chip.-active:focus {
    background: $accent-lit;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   Technical details: present, never competing. Collapsed by default, so the
   raw backend and reproducibility fields stay one keystroke away.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.technical-details {
    height: auto;
    margin: 0 0 1 0;
}

Collapsible {
    background: transparent;
    border: none;
    padding: 0;
    height: auto;
}

Collapsible > CollapsibleTitle {
    color: $ink-3;
    background: transparent;
    padding: 0;
}

Collapsible > CollapsibleTitle:hover,
Collapsible > CollapsibleTitle:focus {
    color: $accent;
    background: transparent;
}

Collapsible > Contents {
    padding: 1 0 0 2;
    height: auto;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   Surfaces. Reach for the lightest one that works.

   .surface-strong  bordered + tinted. Modals and focused inputs. Nothing
                    else — a border around ordinary content is noise.
   .surface-subtle  tinted, no border. Model responses, key/value blocks.
   .section         plain: a title and whitespace. The default.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* The one surface that is genuinely on top of the page. `panel` puts a solid
   accent bar along its top edge, so it reads as a raised window rather than as
   a rectangle someone drew around some text. */
.surface-strong {
    background: $surface;
    border: panel $accent;
    padding: 0 2;
    margin: 0 0 1 0;
    height: auto;
}

.surface-subtle {
    background: $surface-2;
    padding: 0 1;
    height: auto;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   Controls.

   Fields are underlined, not boxed. A rounded rectangle around a text input
   is the default every terminal framework ships, it costs two columns and two
   rows per field, and it says nothing — the app had one around an input that
   was itself inside two more boxes. A rule under the field marks where you
   type, and thickening it to `heavy` on focus makes the focused field
   unmistakable without moving a single cell of layout.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* Textual's Input ships `height: 3` to make room for a border on all four
   edges. With only a bottom rule the other two rows are dead space between
   the text and its own underline. */
Input {
    background: $surface;
    border: none;
    border-bottom: solid $line;
    height: 2;
    padding: 0 1;
    color: $ink;
}
Input:focus {
    border: none;
    border-bottom: heavy $accent;
    background: $surface-3;
}

TextArea {
    background: $well;
    border: none;
    border-bottom: solid $line;
    padding: 0 1;
    color: $ink;
    height: 5;
}

TextArea:focus {
    border: none;
    border-bottom: heavy $accent;
}

Select {
    background: $surface;
    height: auto;
    width: auto;
    min-width: 24;
}

Select > SelectCurrent {
    background: $surface;
    border: none;
    border-bottom: solid $line;
    height: 2;
    padding: 0 1;
    color: $ink;
}

Select:focus > SelectCurrent {
    border: none;
    border-bottom: heavy $accent;
}

/* The open dropdown is a floating surface, so it keeps a full edge — but the
   `tall` half-blocks read as a raised panel rather than as a drawn rectangle. */
Select > SelectOverlay {
    background: $surface-3;
    border: tall $accent;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   Actions.

   Textual's Button is a filled slab: `height: 3` and `min-width: 16`, so a
   four-word toolbar cost three rows and spread "Compare" across sixteen
   columns. Six of those rows on the results screen were blank.

   Secondary actions are bracketed labels; only the primary action is filled.
   All buttons use one row so toolbars remain usable in short terminals.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* The bare Button is the primary's base; every secondary action is an
   `ActionButton`, styled by `.action` below. */
Button {
    background: $line;
    color: $ink;
    border: none;
    height: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    padding: 0 2;
}

Button:hover {
    background: $line-2;
    color: $ink;
}

Button:focus {
    background: $line-2;
    color: $accent;
    text-style: bold;
}

/* Brackets carry the affordance without a filled rectangle. */
Button.action {
    background: transparent;
    color: $ink;
    border: none;
    height: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0 1 0 0;
}

Button.action:hover {
    background: transparent;
    color: $accent-lit;
}

/* Keyboard focus changes shape as well as colour. */
Button.action:focus {
    background: transparent;
    color: $accent;
    text-style: bold underline;
}

/* Disabled actions keep their place and their explanatory tooltips. */
Button.action:disabled {
    background: transparent;
    color: $ink-3;
    text-style: none;
}

/* The one action the screen exists for: a solid slab of accent with near-black
   text on it. No border — on a one-row button an edge would consume the only
   row it has and the label would render into nothing. */
Button.-primary {
    background: $accent;
    color: $on-accent;
    text-style: bold;
    border: none;
    padding: 0 3;
}
Button.-primary:hover,
Button.-primary:focus {
    background: $accent-lit;
    border: none;
    color: $on-accent;
}

/* Unpressable: no face at all — it should not look like something to press.
   Textual dims disabled widgets on top of whatever is set here, so the colour
   starts one step brighter than it ends up. */
Button:disabled {
    background: transparent;
    color: $ink-3;
    border: none;
    text-style: none;
}
Button.-primary:disabled {
    background: transparent;
    color: $ink-3;
    border: none;
}

/* Inside a dense row — a recommendation, a path — a two-row key per action
   would out-shout the row itself, so these are single-row chips. */
Button.-compact {
    height: 1;
    background: $line;
    border: none;
    padding: 0 2;
}
Button.-compact:hover {
    background: $line-2;
}
Button.-compact:focus {
    background: $line-2;
    border: none;
    color: $accent;
    text-style: bold;
}
/* `.-compact` is declared after `.-primary`, so without this the chip's
   neutral face would win and the primary action inside a row would lose its
   accent — the one thing that marks it as the row's main action. */
Button.-primary.-compact {
    background: $accent;
    color: $on-accent;
}
Button.-primary.-compact:hover,
Button.-primary.-compact:focus {
    background: $accent-lit;
    color: $on-accent;
}

Button.-compact:disabled {
    background: transparent;
    color: $ink-3;
}

/* Tertiary action: present, never competing. */
Button.-quiet {
    height: 1;
    border: none;
    padding: 0 2;
    background: transparent;
    color: $ink-3;
}
Button.-quiet:focus, Button.-quiet:hover {
    background: transparent;
    border: none;
    color: $ink-2;
    text-style: underline;
}

RadioSet {
    background: transparent;
    border: none;
    height: auto;
}

Checkbox {
    background: transparent;
    border: none;
}

DataTable {
    background: $surface;
    color: $ink;
    margin: 0 0 1 0;
}
DataTable > .datatable--header {
    background: $line;
    color: $ink-2;
    text-style: bold;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   Warnings, hints, badges.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* A callout, not an enclosure: the amber rectangle outweighed the result it
   was qualifying. The bar marks the edge, the tint carries the meaning. */
.warnings {
    background: $warn-well;
    border: none;
    border-left: outer $warn;
    color: $warn-lit;
    padding: 0 2;
    margin: 0 0 1 0;
    height: auto;
}

/* Inside a warnings panel the heading is part of the warning, not a section
   title in the ordinary hierarchy. */
.warnings .card-title,
.warnings .section-title {
    color: $warn;
}

/* A command to copy reads as a block of code: sunken ground, one edge. */
.cli-hint {
    background: $well;
    border: none;
    border-left: outer $line-2;
    color: $ink-2;
    padding: 0 2;
    margin: 0 0 1 0;
    height: auto;
}

.cli-hint-label {
    color: $ink-3;
    text-style: italic;
}

/* Badges size to their label; without this the background stripe runs the
   full width of the block. */
.badge {
    width: auto;
    padding: 0 2;
    margin: 0 0 1 0;
    text-style: bold;
}
.badge-comfortable        { color: $ok;     background: $ok-tint; }
.badge-compatible         { color: $ok;     background: $ok-tint; }
.badge-tight              { color: $warn;   background: $warn-tint; }
.badge-offloading         { color: $warn;   background: $warn-tint; }
.badge-insufficient       { color: $bad;    background: $bad-tint; }
.badge-unknown            { color: $ink-2; background: $line; }

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   Context bar: the two-line identity + status block every screen opens with.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.context-bar {
    height: auto;
    padding: 0 2;
    margin: 0 0 1 0;
    border-bottom: solid $line;
}

.context-title-row {
    height: 1;
    layout: horizontal;
}

.context-title {
    color: $ink;
    text-style: bold;
    width: 1fr;
    min-width: 16;
    text-wrap: nowrap;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
}

.context-aside {
    color: $ink-3;
    width: auto;
    text-align: right;
    padding: 0 0 0 2;
}

.context-status {
    color: $ink-2;
    height: auto;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   Progress steps.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.step-done    { color: $ok; }
.step-running { color: $accent; text-style: bold; }
.step-pending { color: $ink-3; }
.step-failed  { color: $bad; text-style: bold; }

/* A row whose actions sit on the right edge. */
.actions-right {
    height: auto;
    align-horizontal: right;
    margin: 0 0 1 0;
}

/* A row of actions in reading order, primary first. */
.actions-row {
    height: auto;
    margin: 0 0 1 0;
}

.actions-row Button {
    margin: 0 1 0 0;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   Menus.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.menu-label {
    color: $ink;
    text-style: bold;
}

.menu-hint {
    color: $ink-3;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   Logo.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.logo {
    background: $bg;
    padding: 0 2;
    margin: 0;
    height: auto;
    align: center top;
}
.logo-ascii {
    color: $logo;
    text-style: bold;
    content-align: center middle;
    width: auto;
    height: auto;
    margin-bottom: 0;
}

.logo-subtitle {
    color: $logo-dim;
    text-style: bold;
    content-align: center middle;
    width: auto;
    height: auto;
    margin-top: 0;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   Home screen sea.

   The one widget in the app that is allowed to flex. Everything else is
   sized to its content so the scroll region does the flexing, but this is
   the opposite case: it exists to absorb whatever height is left under the
   menu, and it draws nothing at all when that is zero.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.ocean {
    width: 1fr;
    height: 1fr;
    min-height: 0;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   Search screen patrol.

   Takes whatever height is left under the checklist, and draws the shark at
   a fixed size low down in it rather than filling it — so the swim sits clear
   of the last checklist line on a tall terminal and is simply shrunk on a
   short one, instead of a fixed margin that fits one terminal and overflows
   the next. It draws nothing at all when there is no room left.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.patrol {
    width: 1fr;
    height: 1fr;
    min-height: 0;
}

/* The lane needs the scroll region to have height to give it, so the search
   body flexes where the other bodies are sized to their content. It scrolls
   the same way once the checklist is taller than the terminal. */
#discovery-body {
    height: 1fr;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   Screen bodies. One padding rule rather than one per screen.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
#welcome-body,
#wizard-body,
#hardware-content,
#hardware-summary,
#discovery-body,
#results-body,
#compare-body,
#details-body,
#paths-body,
#path-compare-body,
#validation-body,
#validation-details-body,
#benchmark-body,
#benchmark-details-body,
#scan-content,
#inspect-content,
#doctor-content,
#est-form,
#est-result {
    padding: 0 2;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   Doctor detail.

   What a check found used to be a table cell, which truncated it. In a pane
   it is prose, so it gets a measure: past roughly 70 columns the eye loses
   the start of the next line, and a diagnostic nobody finishes reading is a
   diagnostic that did not run. The body flexes because the workspace inside
   it asks for the height that is left.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
#doctor-content {
    height: 1fr;
}

.doctor-detail-body {
    color: $ink-2;
    padding: 1 0 0 0;
    max-width: 72;
}

#validation-body,
#validation-details-body,
#benchmark-body,
#benchmark-details-body {
    height: 1fr;
}

#hardware-content,
#hardware-summary,
#discovery-messages,
#wizard-errors,
#paths-list,
#path-compare-content,
#results-actions,
#results-actions-secondary,
#discovery-actions,
#wizard-actions,
#paths-actions,
#paths-filters,
#validation-actions,
#benchmark-actions,
#validation-error,
#benchmark-error,
#paths-error,
#path-compare-error,
#res-export-actions {
    height: auto;
}

/* Every action row spaces its buttons the same way. */
#results-actions Button,
#results-actions-secondary Button,
#discovery-actions Button,
#paths-actions Button,
#validation-actions Button,
#benchmark-actions Button,
#res-export-actions Button {
    margin: 0 1 0 0;
}

#validation-actions,
#benchmark-actions,
#paths-actions {
    margin: 0 0 1 0;
}

/* The path screens were the only ones whose ids had no rule at all, so their
   status and empty states inherited nothing. */
#paths-status,
#path-compare-status {
    color: $ink-2;
    height: auto;
}

#paths-empty,
#path-compare-empty {
    color: $ink-3;
    height: auto;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   Welcome.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
#welcome-tagline {
    color: $ink-2;
    padding: 0 0 1 0;
}

#welcome-menu,
#advanced-menu {
    background: transparent;
    height: auto;
}

#welcome-menu > ListItem,
#advanced-menu > ListItem {
    background: transparent;
    padding: 0 0 1 1;
}

#welcome-menu > ListItem.-highlight,
#advanced-menu > ListItem.-highlight {
    background: $surface-3;
}

#welcome-menu > ListItem.-highlight .menu-label,
#advanced-menu > ListItem.-highlight .menu-label {
    color: $accent;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   Requirements wizard: questions separated by whitespace, not by boxes.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* The ordinal is a figure beside the question rather than two characters in
   front of its title: it marks where one question ends and the next begins
   without a rule or a box, and it is the one thing on this screen that can be
   read at a glance from across the room. The spacing is generous on purpose —
   six questions used to be packed tight enough to read as one wall. */
.question {
    height: auto;
    layout: horizontal;
    margin: 0 0 2 0;
}

.question-number {
    width: 5;
    height: auto;
    color: $accent-deep;
    padding: 0 2 0 0;
}

.question-body {
    width: 1fr;
    height: auto;
}

.question-title {
    color: $ink;
    text-style: bold;
}

.question-note {
    color: $ink-3;
    padding: 0 0 0 3;
}

.question > RadioSet,
.question > Checkbox,
.question > Input {
    background: transparent;
    margin: 0 0 0 3;
}

.question > Input {
    width: 60;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   Recommendations.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.rec-list-head {
    height: 1;
    margin: 0 0 1 0;
}

.rec-list-head .section-title {
    width: 1fr;
    padding: 0;
}

.rec-list-aside {
    width: auto;
    text-align: right;
    text-style: bold;
    padding: 0 0 0 2;
    text-wrap: nowrap;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
}

.rec-row {
    height: auto;
    margin: 0 0 1 0;
}

.rec-row:focus-within {
    background: $surface-3;
    outline: solid $line-2;
}

.rec-headline {
    height: 1;
    layout: horizontal;
}

.rec-rank {
    width: 4;
    color: $ink-3;
}

/* The pick the ranking actually made, rather than merely the first row. */
.rec-rank.-top {
    color: $accent;
    text-style: bold;
}

.rec-name {
    width: 1fr;
    min-width: 16;
    color: $ink;
    text-style: bold;
    text-wrap: nowrap;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
}

.selectable.-selected .rec-name {
    color: $accent;
}

/* Runtime, backend and format repeat almost unchanged down the column, so
   they are chrome, not data: they recede a level and let the model name and
   the fit verdict carry the row. The reason is prose meant to be read, and
   stays where the eye can reach it. */
.rec-plan {
    color: $ink-3;
    padding: 0 0 0 4;
    text-wrap: nowrap;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
}

.rec-list-fit { padding: 0 0 0 4; }

.rec-unknown-reason {
    color: $ink-2;
    padding: 0 0 0 4;
}

.rec-actions {
    height: auto;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0 0 1 0;
}

/* Two unframed panes. Narrow terminals retain the same mounted controls and
   switch views; neither pane consumes the other's usable height. */
.selection-workspace { height: 1fr; }
.workspace-switch { height: 1; padding: 0 2; margin-bottom: 1; }
.workspace-switch Button.-active { background: transparent; color: $accent; text-style: bold underline; }
.selection-workspace .workspace-panes { height: 1fr; }
.workspace-master { width: 56%; height: 1fr; padding: 0 2; }
.workspace-detail { width: 44%; height: 1fr; padding: 0 2; border-left: solid $line; }
.selection-workspace.-compact .workspace-master,
.selection-workspace.-compact .workspace-detail { width: 1fr; border: none; }
.selection-workspace.-compact .workspace-switch { margin-bottom: 0; }
.selection-workspace.-compact .inspector-subtitle { margin-bottom: 0; }
.selection-workspace.-compact TabPane { padding: 0; }
.selection-workspace.-compact .section-title { padding-bottom: 0; }
.recommendation-inspector { height: 1fr; }
.inspector-title { text-style: bold; height: auto; }
.inspector-subtitle { color: $ink-2; height: auto; margin-bottom: 1; }
.workspace-detail TabbedContent { height: 1fr; }
.workspace-detail ContentSwitcher { height: 1fr; }
.workspace-detail TabPane { height: 1fr; padding: 1 0; }
.workspace-detail TabPane VerticalScroll { height: 1fr; }
Tab { background: transparent; color: $ink-3; }
Tab.-active { color: $accent; text-style: bold; }
.results-toolbar { height: auto; padding: 0 2; border-top: solid $line; }
.results-criteria { height: auto; padding: 0 2; color: $ink-2; }
.selection-workspace .rec-list-head { height: auto; layout: vertical; }
.selection-workspace .rec-list-aside { width: 1fr; text-align: left; padding: 0; color: $ink-3; }
.selectable.-selected .rec-plan,
.selectable.-selected .path-option-meta,
.selectable.-selected .rec-unknown-reason { color: $ink; }

.rec-actions Button {
    margin: 0 1 0 0;
}

/* A modal should read as something on top of the results, not as a new page. */
ExportReportModal {
    align: center middle;
}

#export-modal {
    background: $surface;
    border: panel $accent;
    padding: 1 2;
    width: 80;
    max-width: 100%;
    height: auto;
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------------
   Tier headings and score rows.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.tier-best-match     { color: $ok; }
.tier-recommended    { color: $accent; }
.tier-closest-option { color: $warn; }
.tier-best-effort    { color: $ink-2; }

.score-total    { color: $ink; }
.score-penalty  { color: $warn; }
.score-row      { color: $ink-2; }

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   Execution paths.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.path-option {
    height: auto;
    margin: 0 0 1 0;
}

.path-option-title {
    color: $ink;
    text-style: bold;
}

.selectable.-selected .path-option-title {
    color: $accent;
}

.path-option-meta {
    color: $ink-2;
    padding: 0 0 0 2;
}

.path-quant {
    height: auto;
    padding: 0 0 0 2;
    margin: 0 0 1 0;
}

.path-quant-label {
    color: $ink-3;
}

/* A dropdown holding quantization names has no business being as wide as the
   terminal; `width: auto` on a Select still fills its container. */
#paths-quant {
    width: 34;
    max-width: 100%;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   Run screen: header, history, composer. History is the dominant zone.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
#run-history {
    height: 1fr;
    padding: 0 2;
}

#run-console {
    height: auto;
    border-top: solid $line;
    padding: 0 2;
}

.run-status {
    color: $ink-2;
    height: 1;
}

.run-error-message {
    color: $warn-lit;
    border-left: solid $warn;
    padding: 0 0 0 1;
    height: auto;
}

#run-composer {
    height: auto;
    padding: 0 0 1 0;
}

#run-composer TextArea {
    width: 1fr;
    height: 4;
}

#run-composer Button {
    width: auto;
    margin: 0 0 0 1;
}

.inference-entry {
    height: auto;
    margin: 0 0 1 0;
}

.inference-prompt {
    border-left: solid $line-2;
    padding: 0 0 0 1;
}

.inference-response {
    background: $surface-2;
    border-left: solid $accent;
    padding: 0 0 0 1;
}

.message-label {
    color: $ink-3;
    text-style: bold;
}

.inference-response .message-label {
    color: $accent;
}

.message-text {
    color: $ink;
}

.message-meta {
    color: $ink-3;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   Validation and benchmark result blocks.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.validation-result-node,
.benchmark-result-node {
    height: auto;
}

.result-headline {
    text-style: bold;
    padding: 0 0 1 0;
}

.result-headline.-ok   { color: $ok; }
.result-headline.-fail { color: $bad; }

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   Advanced tools.

   These were the last screens still drawing a full-width rounded border
   around every block — three nested borders to show one text field in the
   estimate flow. `.card` and `.banner` now resolve to the same flat grouping
   the rest of the app uses, so the advanced tools stay denser and more
   expert-facing without being a visually separate application.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.banner {
    height: auto;
    margin: 0 0 1 0;
    padding: 0 2 1 2;
    border-bottom: solid $line;
}

/* The advanced tools yield their body as an anonymous scroll region, so the
   gutter is applied to the screen rather than to an id. Without it their text
   sat flush against column 0 while every guided screen had a margin — the
   boxes used to supply it. */
AdvancedToolsScreen VerticalScroll,
ScanScreen VerticalScroll,
InspectScreen VerticalScroll,
DoctorScreen VerticalScroll,
EstimateScreen VerticalScroll {
    padding: 0 2;
}

/* The estimate and inspect forms are yielded at screen level, outside any
   scroll region, so they need the gutter themselves. Direct-child only: the
   cards mounted inside an already-padded body must not indent twice. */
EstimateScreen > .card,
InspectScreen > .card {
    padding: 0 2;
}

.banner-title {
    text-style: bold;
    color: $ink;
}

.banner-subtitle {
    color: $ink-2;
}

.card {
    height: auto;
    margin: 0 0 1 0;
}

.card-title {
    text-style: bold;
    color: $ink;
    padding: 0 0 1 0;
}

/* The runnable command keeps a ground, because it is a literal thing to copy
   rather than prose — the one place a box still earns itself here. */
.runtime-card {
    height: auto;
    margin: 0 0 1 0;
}
.runtime-title {
    color: $ink;
    text-style: bold;
    padding: 0 0 1 0;
}
.runtime-code {
    background: $well;
    color: $ink;
    padding: 0 2;
    margin: 0 0 1 0;
}

/* =====================================================================
   TRANSITIONAL.

   Rules below serve markup that has not been moved onto the shared system
   yet. Nothing new should be written against them.
   ===================================================================== */

/* Guided workflow header, superseded by .context-bar. */
.workflow-header {
    height: auto;
    padding: 0 2;
    margin: 0 0 1 0;
    border-bottom: solid $line;
}

.workflow-title {
    color: $ink;
    text-style: bold;
    width: 1fr;
    min-width: 12;
    text-wrap: nowrap;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
}

/* The machine, right-aligned. Truncates before the title does: on a narrow
   terminal the step you are on matters more than the CPU model. */
.workflow-machine {
    color: $ink-3;
    width: auto;
    max-width: 60%;
    text-align: right;
    padding: 0 0 0 2;
    text-wrap: nowrap;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
}

.workflow-breadcrumb {
    color: $ink-2;
    text-wrap: nowrap;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
}

#validation-status,
#benchmark-status {
    color: $ink-2;
    height: auto;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   Validation tabs.

   The outcome, the prediction it is compared against, the path that ran and
   the reproducibility fields used to be a long column plus a second screen.
   As tabs they need the height the body has left, so both flex.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
#validation-result {
    height: 1fr;
}

.validation-tabs {
    height: 1fr;
}

.validation-tabs TabPane {
    height: 1fr;
    padding: 1 0;
}

.validation-tabs TabPane VerticalScroll {
    height: 1fr;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   Hardware headline.

   Two figures, side by side, in seven-segment numerals. Everything this tool
   says is a claim about how much fits in these two numbers, so they are read
   first and from across the room; the unit sits under the figure rather than
   beside it, where it would halve the digits to make room.

   The profile below is a definition list, and a definition list spanning a
   150-column terminal stops being a pair: the measure is capped so the label
   and its value stay in one glance.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.memory-headline {
    height: auto;
    padding: 1 0 2 0;
}

.memory-figure {
    width: auto;
    height: auto;
    padding: 0 6 0 0;
}

.memory-figure-label {
    color: $ink-3;
}

.memory-figure-value {
    color: $accent;
    width: auto;
}

.memory-figure-value.-vram {
    color: $ok;
}

.memory-figure-unit {
    color: $ink-2;
}

#hardware-content {
    max-width: 76;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   Requirements wizard columns.

   Six questions in one column ran past the fold on every terminal, so the
   button that submits them was never on screen with the last answer. Two
   columns halve the height, and the pair is split where the two sides weigh
   about the same rather than down the middle of the list.

   The height has to be stated: a Horizontal defaults to filling its parent,
   which would push the submit button out of the scroll region again.

   Below 100 cells they stack back into one. Two 45-column columns of radio
   labels wrap, and a wrapped option list reads worse than a long one.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.wizard-columns {
    height: auto;
    layout: horizontal;
}

.wizard-column {
    width: 1fr;
    height: auto;
    padding: 0 3 0 0;
}

.wizard-columns.-narrow {
    layout: vertical;
}

.wizard-columns.-narrow .wizard-column {
    width: 1fr;
    padding: 0;
}
